{#-
  salt_user: the user the salt-master daemon runs as (`user:` in
  /etc/salt/master — `salt`, not root, on the Debian packages). Everything
  the daemon has to read itself — the gitfs deploy key, vault.conf, the
  AppRole secret_id — must be owned by or group-readable to this user. Get
  it wrong and the failures read as auth errors, not permission ones.

  git_branch: the repo's default branch. Salt's gitfs_base defaults to
  'master', so on a 'main'-branch repo the branch shows up as an
  environment literally named 'main' and top.sls's `base:` matches nothing
  — states and pillar silently resolve to empty. git_pillar needs the same
  name as its branch-to-env mapping.

  gitfs_remote: must be the explicit ssh:// form, not the scp-like
  `git@host:path` shorthand. pygit2 parses the username out of the URL and
  the shorthand yields None, which it then rejects at remote init
  ("expected str, bytes, or bytearray not <class 'NoneType'>") — and
  because that happens in the master's pre-flight checks, the daemon
  refuses to start at all rather than just disabling gitfs.

  vault_policies: policies saltext.vault assigns to the token it issues
  for a minion context — and pillar rendering counts as one, so this
  applies even though no minion in this repo talks to OpenBao itself. Its
  default (saltstack/minions, saltstack/{minion}) names policies that
  don't exist here, and OpenBao rejects the child token outright:
  "child policies must be subset of parent". Must therefore name a policy
  the master's own AppRole already holds. Narrow this if minions ever get
  their own scoped access.

  git_provider: pygit2. gitpython does not support the privkey/pubkey auth
  params — it logs them as unsupported and then fails to authenticate.
  gitcli authenticates fine and works for gitfs, but git_pillar needs a
  real worktree checkout and gitcli never creates one ("Root path 'pillar'
  not present in git_pillar remote"), so pillar silently stays empty.
  pygit2 is the only provider that does both. It is not importable from
  the distro package because Salt ships as a onedir bundle — install.sls
  installs it into that bundle via salt-pip instead.

  highstate_on_start: reactor on salt/minion/<id>/start runs a full
  state.apply on that minion — a VM fresh from Tofu or just rebooted
  converges without a manual apply. The reactor SLS is served over gitfs
  (reactor_sls). False leaves reactor.conf with an empty mapping.

  mine_consumers: minion IDs whose states read the Salt Mine (prometheus'
  scrape targets). When any minion fires mine_event_tag (its mine conf
  changed), mine_reactor_sls runs a full state.apply on these. Empty =
  no mapping. Repo data, so the list itself lives in pillar/salt-master.sls.

  beacon_restart_services: services the reactor may start again when a
  minion's service beacon (formulas/salt-minion) reports them stopped.
  Whitelist on the master: the event names the service, so without it a
  minion could have the master start anything. Empty = no mapping.

  api_enabled: salt-api (rest_cherrypy) on api_port, HTTPS only, for
  webhooks into the reactor. No external_auth is configured, so /login and
  /run accept nobody — only /hook/<path> works, without Salt auth
  (webhook_disable_auth), and each reactor SLS checks its sender itself.
  api_ssl_crt/api_ssl_key: self-signed, placed by hand (README "salt-api
  TLS"); the state only checks they exist and fixes the key's perms.

  api_alertmanager_token — the bearer token Alertmanager sends to
  /hook/alertmanager (from OpenBao via pillar). Empty = no mapping. Written
  into api.conf as homelab_alertmanager_token for the reactor SLS, so
  api.conf is 0640 like vault.conf.

  known_hosts: host -> {enc, key, port?}, pinned into the system-wide
  ssh_known_hosts for the git remotes gitfs/git_pillar pull from (and the
  hosts they may move to). A dict, so pillar adds hosts on top of GitHub.

  api_git_hmac_secret — shared secret of the git server's push webhook to
  /hook/git (from OpenBao via pillar). The reactor checks the body's
  HMAC-SHA256 (X-Hub-Signature-256) and runs fileserver.update, so a push
  deploys within seconds instead of the next gitfs poll. Empty = no
  mapping. Written into api.conf like the Alertmanager token.

  push_deploy: fileserver_events on, and gitfs' update event (fired after
  every gitfs fetch, `changed` true when new commits arrived) starts
  push_orch_sls (formulas/orch) — "push = deploy" within
  gitfs_update_interval (60 s).
-#}
